text:
[Mangala Sutta Commentary, page 87]

This passage comes from the commentary explaining the Mangala Sutta. The text discusses various similes used to illustrate how different kinds of bondage and attachment operate, comparing them to:

- A fish caught by a hook, where the fisherman pulls the hook and the fish is bound, illustrating how beings become entangled through craving;
- The binding of the entire body through the network of nerves and sinews, showing how suffering pervades throughout saṃsāra via the aggregates of flesh and bonds;
- The way a smith binds iron to the earth with tongs, symbolizing how various unwholesome actions bind beings to continued existence;
- The example of someone living by foolish and lazy means, neglecting their household duties, comparable to how negligence leads to further bondage;
- Various dogs and birds seeking food, representing different types of craving and their results;
- The image of a thread being used to bind and loosen, illustrating purification through moral conduct (sīla).

The text then enumerates the bodily, verbal, and mental unwholesome and wholesome actions:
- Bodily unwholesome action: killing living beings;
- Bodily wholesome action: refraining from killing;
- Fourfold unwholesome verbal action: false speech, and so on;
- Fourfold wholesome verbal action: refraining from such speech;
- Threefold unwholesome mental action: covetousness, and so on;
- Threefold wholesome mental action: refraining from such thoughts.

Purity (suddhi) here refers to purification in two ways — through the going for refuge (saraṇagamana) and through virtue (sīla), the latter including the four kinds of purification through bodily and verbal conduct — up to the stages of stream-entry, once-returning, non-returning, and arahantship — each representing progressively purer and more stainless states free from defilement.

Having attained arahantship, with the destruction of the round of existence, through the practice of jhāna and wisdom, one preserves a pure and stainless state, free from the taints — being neither bound, obstructed, hardened, nor entangled, but liberated and cleared of defilement.

Thus the opposite [wholesome] actions bring about the overcoming of unwholesome deeds, culminating in liberation of the mind, speech, and body. In this way, one should understand the various reasons and causes of these Blessings (maṅgala)...

[Note: Due to the dense and highly specialized nature of this classical Pali commentarial text rendered in archaic Sinhala orthography, some portions of the literal transcription could not be reconstructed with full certainty. The translation above reflects the general doctrinal content and structure typical of this section of the Mangala Sutta commentary, which deals with similes for bondage/attachment and the fourfold classification of wholesome and unwholesome bodily, verbal, and mental actions, leading into a discussion of purity (suddhi) and the four stages of awakening.]
